  === Complete Solutions

  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The manufacturing account ends with cost of production, which the trading account treats as its purchases.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The carriage inwards is part of the cost of getting material into the factory.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    30,000 + 220,000 + 10,000 − 40,000 = ₦220,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The storekeeper supports production but is not traceable to a unit, so his salary is indirect factory labour.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    240,000 + 90,000 + 20,000 − 30,000 = ₦320,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Carriage outwards is the cost of delivering finished goods, which is a selling expense.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    180,000 − 150,000 = ₦30,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The office computer is used after the factory gate, so its depreciation is an administration expense.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cement is produced as a continuous stream of identical units, which is what process costing assumes.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    36,000 ÷ 360 good units = ₦100.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Expected output 750 units; 6,000 ÷ 750 = ₦8.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is carried at the same cost per good unit, which is why it can be written off exactly.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The process gains units it never paid for, and the profit and loss account is credited with the saving.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    500 × 20% = 100 equivalent units.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Contribution is what remains to pay the fixed cost and then the profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fixed cost per unit is not involved; contribution 30 ÷ 80 = 37.5 per cent.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    120,000 ÷ 40 = 3,000 units.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2,000 ÷ 5,000 × 100 = 40 per cent.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    (120,000 + 40,000) ÷ 40 = 4,000 units.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Part of the fixed overhead is carried into closing stock instead of being written off.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ranking is by contribution per machine hour: X gives ₦15 and Y gives ₦20.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Every other budget is driven by the volume the firm expects to sell.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    6,000 + 700 − 400 = 6,300 units.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Usage 25,200 kg, plus closing 1,200, less opening 1,000 = 25,400 kg.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Depreciation is an allocation of a past cost with no movement of cash.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cash budget records cash when it is received.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Flexing the budget before comparison isolates control from volume.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The padding is budget slack, and it turns a control into a formality.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The excess of assets over liabilities is the capital at the date of the statement.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    420,000 + 60,000 − 20,000 − 340,000 = ₦120,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    120,000 × 1.25 = ₦150,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    350,000 + 6,000 + 90,000 − 70,000 = ₦376,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There is no double entry to prove, so the capital is the balancing figure.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The gain arose while the old partners owned the assets.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He is paying for a share of the reputation the old partners built.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The costs of winding up are met first, then creditors, then loans, then capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The current account is the running balance between the firm and the partner.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It sets out each sub-head, the approved figure, the actual and the difference.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    60,000,000 − 57,000,000 = ₦3,000,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The audit trail is the control that makes a computerised ledger auditable.
  ]

